From bf6302e70ea2143f8738b7cec640b48ca366adb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:51:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 64/69] Work out each function once in CheckFastcall

CheckFastcall places the zero page parameters of each function that
takes them above the areas of every function it may call. It walked the
call graph from each function in turn and remembered its result only
for a function that takes fastcall parameters, so a function taking
stack parameters, as every function on a cycle does, was walked again
on every path that reached it: time exponential in the number of
cycles. In a whole-program compile of NetHack the graph it walks
(direct calls, and for a call through a pointer every address-taken
function of its type) has a strongly connected component of 2,465
functions, and it did not finish.

Walk every function once, depth first in the same order, so that each
is decided with the same callees decided before it. What the old walk
read from a function taking stack parameters after walking it again is
the largest area among the fastcall functions it reaches through
functions taking stack parameters, and among the functions still being
walked: FastcallReach finds that by a search, and the value is kept
once the function's component is complete, when no function still
being walked can be reached from it. A dispatch still walks the
functions it calls again before giving them one layout.

A temporary check that ran the old and the new walk from the same
state found the same fastcall and stack call decisions, areas,
parameter offsets, dynamic stack flags and may-call lists for every
function in each of the 1,088 compiles that reached it: 981 of the
autotest sources and nethack64's compiler repros in five
configurations, and 107 NetHack measurement compiles. Only the areas
of functions taking stack parameters, which nothing reads, differ, in
two C++ programs. It agreed as well in 1,600 compiles of 400 random
programs with cycles, calls through pointers and setjmp. All 988
corpus programs that both compilers build are byte for byte the same.

